Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||10-08-2000||01:45 AM||||Anyone here ever built a 1930's era hot rod? specifically, a 1930 Ford Model A Sedan.<BR>I have some questions, please email me..<P>thanks!!!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||10-08-2000||07:58 AM||||Yeah but I don't think I would be much help did this about 40 yrs. ago<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/car.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||10-08-2000||02:13 PM||||What are the questions? I may not be able to help but you never know. I have done my 40 Ford and 41 Chevy and I think I know alot about A Models...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||10-08-2000||05:01 PM||||We are building a 40 Ford coupe and a 47 four door sedan. This is a new thing for us, so we are learning as we go. Maybe someone here can help you- and me!||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||10-08-2000||06:09 PM||||Actually, any 1930-1935 sedan will do, I mentioned the Model A because those were the kinds I saw yesterday.<P>basically my questions are, approximately how much would a restorable (no interior, no glass, non-working engine etc) 2dr sedan cost? and also, how easy are they to work on?<BR>(new engine, new brakes & suspension, etc)<P>thanks!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Songman||10-08-2000||08:35 PM||||My 40 Ford is a 2-door Sedan DeLuxe. It is an older restoration and is sorely in need of being redone again but has gotten to the back burner because of all of my projects. My biggest one now is my Monte Carlo SS Aerocoupe. It is getting a 454 crate in place of the stock 305. Going totally NASCAR with it.<P>In one of Fiero posts today I made the statement that a Fiero seems to be worth whatever someone is willing to pay for it. Prices cary so much that it is hard to keep up with. I just bought an 88 Formula for $500 last week. And a few months ago I traded an 85 Cadillac Biarritz convertible for an 88 coupe! I think you'll find this when looking for a street rod project too. You'll find people who have outrageous prices and if you keep on looking, eventually you'll find one at a decent price.<P>My opinion on this is that if I could find a car that was in good enough shape I would fix it original. It would have to be rough for me to build a rod out of it. A rolling chassis Model A to me would be worth anywhere from $1000-$2500 depending. This means no motor, no interior, but good chrome or at least some of it...<P>I have been after a 36 Packard Limo for quite a while now. It belongs to my neighbor back down in GA. It sits in his building so it is not deteriorating. It is like I said above: no motor, interior, etc. But all the metal is there even though there is some rust and dents. We haven't settled on a price yet but I'm figuring as high as $3500, but what a street rod this big ole thing will make!<P>Keep us informed on your search.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-08-2000||09:00 PM||||Our 47 Ford with a good running flat head v8 costed us about $1500. It is too rough to be a good restoration, so we are going with a tuned port 350, 700r4 and narrowed 9 inch with four wheel disk and independent front suspension. The 40 Ford will also get a 350, 9 inch rear end, but a carb and 350 auto-the owner doesn't like all the electronics of a F.I. driveline.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||10-09-2000||12:02 AM||||Check out <A HREF="http://www.yogisinc.com" TARGET=_blank>Yogi's</A>.  They have a ton of stuff for 30's restorations.  Chassis, full rolling chassis, suspensions, parts, motors, wiring kits, patch panels, interior pieces, door handles, digital dashes, grilles, window seals, hood panels, HVAC units you name it.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">          <BR> <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Songman||10-10-2000||04:57 AM||||There you go! Tigger gave you your answer! If it is still there, this looks like a deal to me.... taken from the Classified section of Yogi's ..and I see you are in Canada too!<P>SELL: '30-31 Model "A" tudor body, frames, '28 front fenders, '31 fenders, headlight bars & buckets, many other misc. parts. $850. cdn obo for the lot.<BR> Alberta Canada<BR> Terry Holt, 403 638-2064, bankrust@telusplanet.net<BR> This ad was posted 22-Sep-00||||reg||1
Z||000009||Songman||10-12-2000||12:35 AM||||Did anyone call about this deal on the Model A? I was just wondering if it was any good.||||reg||5
Z||000010||DaRkLoRD||10-12-2000||12:49 AM||||This project is so many years away, the only things I've done so far besides posting this question... is gone to a car show, and bought 2 hot rod magazines.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The first priority is the Fiero.. I'll worry about other cars later. hehe.. there's a lot of changes I'd make to the stock systems of the 1930s car, so I might end up going with a fiberglass kit, on a tube chassis. more expensive, but much less work to retrofit modern parts into an old car.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Tigger||10-12-2000||11:17 AM||||Steve, if your interested in kits, have you seen the Speedstar coupe kit?<P>Guy near me has one and it has one mean rake to it.  It looks sweet with 20" AR Torque Thrust II rims on the back and 17" on the fronts.  $9000 smacks, gives you the body with doors hung, locks, power windows, and frame.  Still leaves a lot of work that has to be done.  I think they offer turn key cars, but I'd have to check the price. (maybe $40,000)<P>I know it's out of most of our price ranges (including mine), but I was just wondering if you've seen that kit.||||reg||1
